    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.comment\n\n### Description\n\nThe plugin fails to account for the fact that for filetype `asciidoc` (assuming a `'commentstring'` of `//%s`), a line can be considered a comment only when the `//` is flush left.\r\n\r\nThus, if `//` occurs indented on a line, that line is _not_ a comment, so invoking `gcc` on that line should add another `//`, not remove the existing one.\r\n\r\nI've pushed a commit https://github.com/ds26gte/mini.comment/commit/a19c2bceffc98383c6cca2563c9d47e19ae4925a to my fork of your repo. Please inspect and consider for inclusion after suitable modification. Thanks.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.7.2\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Install mini.comment repo and setup anyway you choose\r\n2.  Open an empty file j.adoc\r\n3.  At the neovim command line, type `setl cms=//%s`\r\n4.  Add a line `  ` ` ` ` ` ` ` `abc`  (i.e., with some leading spaces)\r\n5.  Call `gcc` on that line\r\n6.  You fill find that the added `//` is indented, which is not a valid AsciiDoc comment\r\n7.  Type `gcc` again. You will find that the `//` is removed, even though the line is not a valid AsciiDoc comment\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nTyping `gcc` on an indented line should add a new `//` flush left, not an indented `//`.\r\n\r\nTyping `gcc` on an indented line with leading `//` should not remove that `//`, but should a new `//` flush left.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nTyping `gcc` on an indented line that doesn't lead with `//` preserves the indent and adds a `// ` . (Adding the `//` is correct, indenting it is wrong.)\r\n\r\nTyping `gcc` on an indented line with a leading `//` removes the `//`.",
